For large, regular inputs that map to small outputs, do not guess a panel mask, center sample, or crop from dimensions alone. Enumerate plausible source coordinates (periodic row/column indices, intersections, exceptional bands) and align each output cell against the demo. Record source-value tuples and output values, including counterexamples where candidate coordinates agree but outputs differ. Validate every output cell in every demo before applying a mapping.

After a rejected submission, discard the whole candidate; do not make a speculative near-variant. If the demo cap is exhausted, revisit output dimensions and transformation class rather than treating the remaining guesses as justified. Submit only a candidate supported by the complete demo set; preserve one-action JSON protocol.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u096/system/state/hermes/skills/arc/arc-demo-evidence-processing/references/compact-output-after-cap.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-recovery", "file": "references/sparse-marker-selection.md", "content": "# Sparse-marker rule selection: lesson from task-2070c748

When demonstrations show color 2 appearing in some horizontal and vertical gaps between same-color markers, that establishes only a possible geometric operation, not the criterion selecting which lines are transformed. Do not blanket-fill all aligned intervals in the test: several broad interval/ray submissions were rejected in this recurrence despite demos containing some filled gaps. Later demos show selected horizontal/vertical line gaps, but the selection rule remains unresolved unless all changed and unchanged examples can be explained together.

Recovery procedure: enumerate exact changed coordinates for every demo, then classify candidate lines by their marker arrangement and note comparable unchanged lines. Keep selection hypotheses explicit. After a wrong submission, discard that full hypothesis; do not submit a nearby broader variant. Request another demo only when its potential outcomes would separate specific selection hypotheses and alter the predicted test grid. Apply any proposed rule cellwise to all demos before submitting. See the parent skill's workflow for general rejection and demo discipline.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u096/system/state/hermes/skills/arc/arc-demo-recovery/references/sparse-marker-selection.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/seeded-region-selection-caution.md", "content": "# Seeded region selection: validate before extrapolating

A recurring ARC pattern may recolor background regions associated with a seed, but the visual presence of a seed and large recolored areas does not establish ordinary flood fill. On task-d2414d9a, the test contained background 6, marker 5, and a 3×3 seed-like cluster. Several plausible candidates—4-neighbor or 8-neighbor propagation through 0 or 6, and broad background recoloring—were not validated and repeated submissions failed. No reliable selection rule was found.

For future tasks of this class, derive the exact target mask from input/output differences in each demonstration. Compare transformed and untransformed cells of the same background color; test boundary/enclosure, seed-relative geometry, and connectivity separately. Implement each candidate and replay complete demos before applying it to the test. When no candidate reproduces every demo, do not describe it as validated.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u096/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzles/arc-grid-reasoning/references/seeded-region-selection-caution.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/tiny-test-scaling.md", "content": "# Tiny-test scaling ambiguity

A uniform or very small test can be consistent with many transformations. Do not assume its eventual output will preserve the test dimensions or simply repeat the input. Derive output shape and tiling from demos, and separately identify the contents/orientation of each repeated tile. In particular, a demo can reveal a repeat factor without showing that the repeated tile is the original input: compare the tile cell by cell. If the rule's tile transform matters on the test but is underdetermined by current examples, request a demo that distinguishes plausible alternatives rather than treating an easy symmetric answer as proof of the general rule.

The task-5fb258d3 demo was 6×8→18×16, while the test contained two identical color-2 cells vertically. The submitted 6×2 all-2 answer was accepted, but this tiny uniform case did not establish a general rule for transforming the larger demo's nonuniform tile. Reuse only the conservative lesson: validate output dimensions and the transformation independently; do not extrapolate a broad repetition rule from this instance.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u096/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzles/arc-grid-reasoning/references/tiny-test-scaling.md"}

