This run's early hypothesis was 3x3 center sampling with center value 5 mapped to 0. It failed repeatedly, including after the recurring-task center-sample note was consulted. Later demos included 6x6→2x2 and grids with non-5 backgrounds, which do not fit a blanket 5-to-0 center-sample rule: e.g., the 6x6 example's output `[[0,0],[3,7]]` matches sampling the centers of the four 3x3 blocks, preserving those center values (and incidentally mapping sampled 5 values to 0 in that pair). The 15x15 test's center samples, using rows/cols 1,4,7,10,13, are `[[0,5,4,0,7],[4,5,5,4,4],[7,5,5,7,7],[4,5,4,4,4],[7,7,0,4,0]]`. All variants based on mapping 5 to 0 were rejected. Treat the exact 5 mapping as unresolved; do not repeat it without evidence. The session exhausted demos and ended unsolved, so this note records a rejected hypothesis, not a validated solution.

General lesson: one known transformation may share the same output dimensions and sampling coordinates while a palette-dependent mapping differs. Validate mapping independently from spatial extraction across every demo, especially examples where sampled cells equal the disputed color.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u096/system/state/hermes/skills/arc/arc-feedback-recovery/references/task-197199f9-session.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-processing", "file": "references/rejected-submission-learning.md", "content": "# Rejected submissions: recover before trying again

After an incorrect ARC output, reject the full proposed transformation, not only its parameter (for example, a fill's color). First record exactly which cells the attempt changed and which regions it preserved. Then re-read the demo input/output differences and compare their full changed-cell sets to the attempted operation. A demo may contain independent effects in several distant objects; implementing only the visually obvious local effect is not a validated rule.

Do not spend consecutive submissions on nearly identical variants (same geometry with a different fill color, or same fill with a slightly altered extent) unless a new example specifically supports that spatial operation and resolves the previous mismatch. Prefer a targeted demo when competing rules predict different test outputs. Before submitting, require one rule to reproduce all demonstrated changes and non-changes cell-by-cell. Keep the protocol constraint in view: exactly one JSON action per response.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u096/system/state/hermes/skills/arc/arc-demo-evidence-processing/references/rejected-submission-learning.md"}

